= Development =

This file contains contributor information for the Rust workspace. End-user
operation and the compatibility contracts are documented in `doc/WIILAND`.

== Repository ==

The project is developed in the Git repository:
  https://github.com/PhilosophiMoonbeam/wiiland

The related Linux device-driver development is upstream:
  https://git.kernel.org/
Discussion takes place on:
  linux-input@vger.kernel.org

The protocol and device captures in `doc/PROTOCOL`, `doc/DEVICES`,
`doc/DEV_REMOTE`, `doc/eeprom.dump`, and `doc/rdesc.dump` are archival
references. They are not generated build inputs.

== Workspace ==

The repository pins Rust 1.98.0 in `rust-toolchain.toml`.  The Cargo workspace
uses resolver 3, edition 2024, and package version 2.0.0.  The principal
packages are:

* `crates/wiiland-hid` — native Rust ownership, decoding, and control of
  devices exposed by Linux `hid-wiimote`; consumers use its root facade.
* `crates/wiiland-core` — deterministic event engine, configuration parser,
  mapping, pointer, aim, and trace logic, plus a separate config I/O adapter.
* `crates/wiilandd` — the `wiilandd` daemon and
  `wiilandd-hardware-report`.
* `crates/wiiland-ipc` — source-level, `rlib`-only Rust DTOs and blocking
  versioned Unix-socket `Client` for daemon consumers.
* `crates/wiiland-config` — optional eframe/egui Wayland/X11 control center.
* `crates/wiiland-show` — optional ratatui/crossterm diagnostic TUI.
* `crates/wiiland-dump` — non-installed EEPROM diagnostic.
* `xtask` — typed build, install, documentation, and distribution workflows.

The supported integration boundary has two mutually exclusive ownership paths:
an application may add `wiiland-hid` as a source-level Cargo path dependency
and own Linux HID interfaces directly, or it may add `wiiland-ipc` as a
source-level path dependency and use `Client` while `wiilandd` owns hardware.
The README contains concise examples using the public root facades. Do not
depend on raw decoder/model modules or kernel implementation details.

`wiiland-ipc::Client::connect` negotiates protocol major version automatically.
`connect_default` targets the private per-user
`$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/wiiland/wiilandd.sock` endpoint only for an absolute runtime
directory. There is no global system socket. These source-level integration
crates are not installed development libraries or standalone clients:
packaging must not emit C headers, development libraries, static/shared
libraries, pkg-config metadata, or a client executable. Neither path promises
a stable Rust ABI or a C ABI.

The daemon remains display-neutral: device ingestion and uinput output do not
call a display-server API. The GUI's winit backend is selected by the active
Wayland/X11 environment, while the TUI owns and restores its terminal.

See `doc/ARCHITECTURE.md` for event processing, capture ownership, bounded
diagnostics, and the additive IPC 1.1 commands.

== Build ==

Use the repository's Cargo alias for the xtask package:

  cargo xtask build --features gui,tui,integrations
  cargo xtask build --release --features gui,tui,integrations
  cargo xtask check --all-features
  cargo xtask docs

The `gui`, `tui`, and `integrations` feature names select the optional
frontends and installed integration assets where supported. `cargo xtask build`
places profile outputs in `target/debug` or `target/release`.

The focused workspace checks are:

  cargo fmt --check
  cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets --all-features --locked -- -D warnings
  cargo test --workspace --all-features --locked

Keep device ingestion aligned with the kernel `hid-wiimote` contract: udev and
sysfs topology, exact evdev node names and codes, LEDs, rumble, and
`SYN_DROPPED` recovery. The project does not publish a C ABI.

== Install and distribution ==

Build the selected profile before installing it. `xtask` installs from the
corresponding Cargo target directory:

  cargo xtask build --release --features gui,tui,integrations
  cargo xtask install --release --prefix /usr --sysconfdir /etc \
    --features gui,tui,integrations

For packaging, pass `--destdir PATH`; it prefixes staged destinations without
appearing in generated service files:

  cargo xtask install --release --destdir "$DESTDIR" \
    --prefix /usr --sysconfdir /etc \
    --features gui,tui,integrations

Logical directory options include `--prefix`, `--exec-prefix`, `--bindir`,
`--datadir`, `--docdir`, and `--mandir`. `--sysconfdir` is accepted only as
`/etc`, matching the runtime's canonical system configuration path.
Integration destinations independently accept
`auto`, `no`, or an absolute path:
`--with-udev-rules-dir`, `--with-systemd-user-unit-dir`, and
`--with-xorg-conf-dir`. `cargo xtask uninstall` reads its ownership marker and
removes only unchanged files owned by that install.

The deterministic source archive workflow is:

  cargo xtask dist --output wiiland-2.tar.xz
  cargo xtask verify-dist wiiland-2.tar.xz

`dist` creates one `wiiland-2` archive root and adds `verify-dist.sh`;
`verify-dist` rejects unsafe or extra roots, extracts the archive, and runs its
locked Cargo workspace check.

